@using Memoria.Web.Extensibility

@* The note beside a stored version saying that events have been appended since the row was
   written. One component rather than a copy on each of the four detail pages: the same fact is
   true of a streamed aggregate, a streamed projection, a DCB aggregate and a DCB projection, and a
   reader who meets it on one of them should meet the same sentence and the same mark on the next.

   Inline and beside the value it is about rather than a banner above the panel. What has come
   apart is one number on one row, and a note at the top of the tab would leave a reader looking
   down the list for which of a dozen facts it meant.

   A caution in the page's own sense of the word: nothing failed and nothing is wrong with the row.
   A snapshot is a fold of the history as it stood, and a history that has moved on since is the
   ordinary working state of an event-sourced store rather than a fault in it. *@
<span class="behind">
    @* Built to the box, stroke and linecap every other mark here is built to, so a warning beside a
       value is drawn at the weight of the marks beside the headings. Hidden from screen readers:
       the sentence after it says what it means, and read out it would be the same thing twice. *@
    <svg class="glyph" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
         fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
         stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
        @* A clock, because what both sentences say is that time has moved on past the stored row:
           the history has grown since it was written, or a version was reached that it has not.
           Not the triangle with a mark in it that stood here before — that is the drawing of a
           fault, and the sentence beside it is at pains to say nothing failed. *@
        <circle cx="10" cy="10" r="7"/>
        <path d="M10 6.2V10l2.6 2"/>
    </svg>
    @* The fact, then the way to act on it as a link into the sentence that says what acting would
       do: a reader told the row is behind is one step from putting it right, and the step is the
       update tab. In the same ink as the words around it, underlined so it still reads as a way
       on rather than as emphasis. *@
    @Note.Text
    <a href="@UpdateHref">Update the snapshot</a> @Note.Remedy
</span>

@code {

    /// <summary>The note to set beside the value: the fact, and what updating would do about it.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The note rather than the numbers it is worked out from: whether there is anything to warn
    /// about is <see cref="SnapshotLag.Of"/>'s answer on an info tab and
    /// <see cref="UnappliedVersion.Of"/>'s on a compare tab, so a page that has one of these has
    /// already established that it has something to say — and this draws it without deciding it
    /// again.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public SnapshotNote Note { get; set; } = new(string.Empty, string.Empty);

    /// <summary>The address of the page's update tab, which is where the remedy is carried out.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string UpdateHref { get; set; } = string.Empty;

}
